time flies when you're having fun and  though it's hard to believe i've now  been putting videos up on this channel  for two whole years more than a thousand  videos  more than 5 000 subscribers and this  thing has become far bigger than i ever  predicted  now after big milestone i always think  it's kind of important to look back  take stock and think about what i've  learned in the last 12 months i think  the biggest thing i've discovered or  i should say rediscovered has definitely  been  make mistakes you know make mistakes and  don't be afraid  of making mistakes as a math teacher i  spend all day asking questions  many people think that you know teaching  is mainly about telling people what to  think  that's kind of true but more often i'm  asking questions and trying to draw  students toward articulating ideas and  developing skills themselves  and a long time ago very wise person  told me that the one doing the talking  is the one doing the learning  which is why peer teaching is so  important but  i'm getting sidetracked my point is that  i ask lots of questions every day  and so often i find students preface  their answers with a little phrase  and that phrase it breaks my heart every  time i hear it  the phrase is i'm not sure this is right  but look i'm exaggerating a little i'm  actually really glad that someone anyone  would be willing to stick their hand up  in class  even when they're not 100 confident  they're right that's awesome and  actually i'm really proud of my students  when they go out on a limb with an idea  and take a risk in front of their  friends i think that takes a lot of  courage and bravery and that's fantastic  right  but i think it'd say is it reveals  something really sad  about classroom culture and our society  in general  when we feel like we kind of need to ask  for permission before we say  something when we're not sure it's 100  right and kind of we're all like that  are we you know we put up our hand and  even though it's a very simple physical  action  it almost feels like in a classroom  there's this big uh  stage light sort of shining on us and  now everyone's looking at us and if we  get things  wrong then everyone will think we're an  idiot i think it's really sad  that our classrooms are like that and  that our society is like that  that there's so much uh expectation and  pressure  that we have to get things right and we  have to get them right first time  in fact it's when we get things wrong  it's when we make mistakes  and then analyze those and try and work  out why we did things wrong  that's when we actually learn so this  year as many of you know  i've been in a new role in a new school  and  i've been constantly every day multiple  times a day  thrust into unfamiliar situations and  i've made mistakes  and here's the important thing that i've  really discovered rediscovered  this year when you're under pressure  when you don't have enough time to get  everything done  when you're encountering a new situation  that's when you really  learn and it's important to put yourself  deliberately  into those situations getting outside  your comfort zone  is really hard if you're anything like  me then you've kind of spent your whole  life  running away from mistakes and there's  kind of this analogy that i really like  which is that everyone has a mistake  monster it's kind of like you know this  um little creature that's following us  around and we spend  you know so much of our effort trying to  flee from that monster  as if it's gonna you know pin us to the  ground and sort of eat our brains out  when it catches us  and we're very very like we're afraid  we're mortified  of making mistakes and making them in  front of other people but the reality is  that our mistake  monsters everyone's got one um is  actually if you can make it your friend  going to be a really valuable ally  because our mistake monsters they just  want to teach us they want us to help  they want to help us learn what's a good  way to do things what's an ineffective  way to do things  and why one things are one way or  another  if only we will learn to embrace our  mistakes and say well  okay i failed i'll work out why i failed  and i'll make sure that i don't do that  next time then that'll free us up to do  all kinds of different things that we  would just be too risk-averse and too  scared to ever attempt  there are always going to be people  around who dislike you getting things  wrong  is not only ourselves and our own desire  to always be seen as  you know getting things right and having  this wonderful self-image  but there will be maybe you know your  family or the people that you work with  or the people who supervise you  you're always going to interact with  some people who are very intolerant of  people making mistakes  but it's really up to you to settle for  yourself  the person who's really got to live with  your mistakes is you  and if you decide if you resolve to say  look i'm going to make mistakes in fact  i'm going to  attempt things and put myself in myself  in situations where  i know i'm going to do things wrong and  i'm going to have to pick myself up  then you're going to learn lots more  you're going to achieve a lot more  and that's going to be beneficial to you  and to everyone around you  so here's some homework for you i want  you to go away and have a careful think  to yourself  about something that you know you've  been thinking about doing you've been  seeing other people doing perhaps  but you've always kept yourself back  from because you're just too scared you  know you're gonna probably screw up and  you know you don't want to put yourself  in that situation you don't want to see  um other people recognize that you're  not perfect  that you can make mistakes think about  something like that  and resolve that you're going to give it  a go you're going to give it a shot  and if you mess up which you probably  will that's okay  you're going to pick yourself up you're  going to learn from that and you're  going to give it another shot  and you're going to keep on persevering  until you really master it  do it for yourself it's going to be  really valuable you're going to learn  tons from it  and your character will grow as well and  good luck
 

